  explanation: '**"Loss of weight in spite of good appetite" is thyrotoxicosis written out in full, and `L10) orbit` slide 15 prints that exact symptom first.** Its list of Graves symptoms reads: "**weight loss despite good appetite**, increased bowel frequency, sweating, heat intolerance, nervousness, irritability, palpitations, weakness and fatigue." The same slide adds that Graves disease "is **more common in females**", which the patient’s sex and age support — thyroid eye disease characteristically appears in women in their third to fifth decades.\n\n**The eye findings fit too, including the unilateral one.** `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` states that thyroid eye disease is "the most common cause of **unilateral or bilateral** protrusion of the globes, or exophthalmos" — the deck writes "unilateral" first precisely because a one-sided proptosis so often prompts a hunt for a tumour. `L10` slide 16 covers the soft-tissue involvement that produces the chemosis and congestion here: "**grittiness, red eyes, lacrimation, photophobia, puffy lids and retrobulbar discomfort**", with Fig. 4.10 showing "**epibulbar hyperaemia overlying a horizontal rectus muscle**" and "periorbital oedema, **chemosis** and prolapse of fat into the eyelids."\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong** — and note that each is excluded by something the stem does or does not say:\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute orbital cellulitis** | The stem gives **no fever, no pain, no sinusitis and no acute onset**. `L10` slide 26 requires "**Malaise and Pyrexia, often marked**", "tender, firm, erythematous and **warm** eyelids" and "**painful** ophthalmoplegia", with infection typically from the **ethmoid** sinuses. This patient’s history is systemic and chronic, not febrile and sudden. |\n| **Cavernous sinus thrombosis** | Would be a catastrophe, not an outpatient consultation. `L10` slide 32 describes "**rapid onset**" with "severe headache, malaise, nausea and vomiting", proptosis "unilateral or often **BILATERAL**", cranial nerves **III to VI** affected, and a mortality of "20 % treated and up to 100 % untreated". None of that is present. |\n| **Lacrimal gland tumor** | The wrong *shape* of proptosis and the wrong history. `L10` slide 42 gives pleomorphic adenoma as "**PAINLESS SLOWLY PROGRESSIVE** proptosis or swelling in the **superolateral** eyelid, usually of **MORE THAN A YEAR’S DURATION**", with **S-shaped ptosis** and a **downward and inward dystopia** — the eye is pushed *down and in*, not straight forward — and no systemic symptoms. Slide 43 gives lacrimal gland carcinoma (**adenoid cystic 50 %**) with rapid-onset proptosis and **bony erosions** on CT. Neither causes weight loss with a good appetite. |\n\n**What to look for to confirm it clinically**, from `L10` slides 16–20 — the lecturer’s own five headings:\n\n1. **Soft tissue** — grittiness, red eyes, lacrimation, photophobia, puffy lids (present in this patient as chemosis and congestion).\n2. **Lid retraction**, with the three eponyms on slide 17: **Dalrymple** sign (lid retraction), **Kocher** sign (severe bilateral retraction) and **von Graefe** sign (**lid lag on downgaze**). `L23` describes the same as "retraction of the upper and lower eyelid, with **upper-eyelid lag on downgaze**." **Lid retraction is the sign a tumour cannot produce**, and it is what settles the diagnosis at the bedside.\n3. **Proptosis** — measured on a Hertel exophthalmometer; slide 8 gives "readings greater than **20 mm**" and "a difference of **2–3 mm or more** between the two eyes."\n4. **Restrictive myopathy** — diplopia; the inferior rectus first (`opqb-t3-137`).\n5. **Optic neuropathy** — the sight-threatening complication, monitored by "visual acuity, colour desaturation, relative afferent pupillary defect and visual field defects **regularly**" (slide 20).\n\nThe systemic examination on slide 15 completes it: "enlargement of the thyroid gland, tremor, palmar erythema and warm and sweaty skin", with "sinus tachycardia and other arrhythmias."\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Confirm with thyroid function tests and treat the thyroid, but treat the eye on its own terms. `L10` slide 22 puts **"Stop smoking"** first and largest in mild disease — the lecturer’s own emphasis, and the single most effective intervention, because smoking both worsens the orbitopathy and blunts the response to treatment — followed by lubricants, topical anti-inflammatories, **head elevation on three pillows** and **eyelid taping** at night. Slide 23 escalates moderate-to-severe active disease to systemic steroids (intravenous methylprednisolone for compressive optic neuropathy), orbital steroid injections, low-dose fractionated radiotherapy and orbital wall decompression, with squint and lid surgery **later**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Systemic Diseases — the diagnosis is made from the systemic history.)*',
  objective: 'Weight loss with a good appetite plus unilateral proptosis and chemosis in a young woman is thyroid eye disease; lid retraction is the sign no orbital tumour produces.',
